================================================================================
THIRD-PARTY TERMS — THE MIT LICENCE ABOVE COVERS THE CODE, NOT THESE
================================================================================

Two obligations survive the MIT licence. Neither is optional, and the second one
attaches to what the application SAYS OUT LOUD, not merely to this repository.


1. THE AVATAR IS NON-COMMERCIAL. REPLACING IT IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The avatar this project ships with as a working default was created at Ready
Player Me and is distributed by the TalkingHead project under

    Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International (CC BY-NC 4.0)

Personal, educational and other non-commercial use is permitted with credit. Any
commercial use is not — not "probably fine", not "with attribution".

READ THIS PART BEFORE ASSUMING YOU CAN FIX IT BY MAKING YOUR OWN. Ready Player Me
retains the intellectual property in avatars created through their platform, and
avatars made on their website are themselves licensed CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 —
non-commercial AND ShareAlike, which is stricter than the default avatar's terms,
not looser. Swapping in an avatar you made yourself therefore does NOT remove the
restriction; it may tighten it.

For non-commercial use — which includes personal study, the case this project was
built for — the shipped avatar is fine as it stands. Credit it.

For commercial use you need one of:

  * registration as a Ready Player Me Partner, which they offer free of charge
    and which is what grants commercial rights to RPM avatars; or
  * an avatar from a source whose terms you have read and that permits your use.
    A CC0 or self-authored model (Blender, MakeHuman/MPFB) carries no such
    condition at all; note that TalkingHead's own CC0 example, mpfb.glb, is
    missing the viseme_sil blendshape and will not close its mouth, so verify any
    candidate with `python -m backend.tools.check_avatar` before relying on it.

Replacing the avatar is mechanically trivial — the persona file names its own GLB
(`<!-- avatar: name.glb -->`), so it is one file:

    1. Obtain a full-body GLB with a Mixamo-compatible rig
    2. It must carry BOTH morph target groups, ARKit (52) and Oculus visemes (15).
       From Ready Player Me that means:
       https://models.readyplayer.me/<YOUR_ID>.glb?morphTargets=ARKit,Oculus%20Visemes
    3. Save it under the name the persona declares, e.g. frontend/public/minami.glb
    4. Verify it:  python -m backend.tools.check_avatar --all

The MIT licence above grants you no rights whatsoever to any avatar. If you fork
this repository and ship it commercially, the avatar is your responsibility to
license, and this is not legal advice — read the terms yourself.


2. VOICEVOX REQUIRES ATTRIBUTION WHEREVER THE VOICE IS HEARD
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The synthesised voice comes from VOICEVOX. Its character voices permit
commercial and non-commercial use, but ONLY if the character is credited. The
credit is per character, not per engine, so it depends on which persona is
configured (TUTOR_PERSONA):

    tanaka  (default)  VOICEVOX:麒ヶ島宗麟
    minami             VOICEVOX:No.7
    hayashi            VOICEVOX:栗田まろん
    mori               VOICEVOX:冥鳴ひまり

Show the credit wherever the audio appears — in the application, in a video, in
a description. Adding a persona means checking that character's own terms; they
are set per character by their respective rights holders and are not uniform.


